</div>
<div id="A1.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now that we have the respective variances and covariances, we can build the positive definite covariance matrix for <math id="A1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}({\bar{\mathcal{D}}}(r^{0}_{j})=\text{vec}(R^{0}_{j})|r^{t}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>vec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}({\bar{\mathcal{D}}}(r^{0}_{j})=\text{vec}(R^{0}_{j})|r^{t}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, which at diffusion step <math id="A1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}^{2}\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}^{2}\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To show that <math id="A1.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive definite matrix, we use Sylvesters criterion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gilbert (1991)</a></cite>.
It states that a symmetric matrix <math id="A1.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite if each upper left corner matrix of sizes <math id="A1.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{1,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{1,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> has a positive determinant.
Since <math id="A1.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a relatively large matrix of size <math id="A1.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\times 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\times 9</annotation></semantics></math>, we use SymPy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meurer et al. (2017)</a></cite> to compute each corner matrix determinant.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">We find that each determinant equals a positive number that depends on <math id="A1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first <math id="A1.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> corner matrices trivially have determinant 1 since they are all identity matrices.
The determinant for <math id="A1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\{7,8,9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\{7,8,9\}</annotation></semantics></math> (termed <math id="A1.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{n\times n}_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{n\times n}_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>) are the following:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{det}(\Sigma^{7\times 7}_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}})=2w_{t}^{2}\qquad\text{det}(\Sigma^{8\times 8}_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}})=(2w_{t}^{2})^{2}\qquad\text{det}(\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}})=(2w_{t}^{2})^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{det}(\Sigma^{7\times 7}_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}})=2w_{t}^{2}\qquad\text{det}(\Sigma^{8\times 8}_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}})=(2w_{t}^{2})^{2}\qquad\text{det}(\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}})=(2w_{t}^{2})^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">thus proving that <math id="A1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since we assume every rotation in <math id="A1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{t}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{t}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of each other, when we combine the <math id="A1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}^{2}\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}^{2}\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each rotation, we get a large positive definite matrix <math id="A1.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}^{2}\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}^{2}\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we can approximate the distribution <math id="A1.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathcal{D}(r^{0}_{M})|r^{t}_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathcal{D}(r^{0}_{M})|r^{t}_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> as a Gaussian using the mean and the derived positive definite matrix.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As stated earlier, we use BoDiffusion as the base Diffusion model for <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">InPose</span>.
The conditional inputs for CFG are the joint rotations, locations, the joint velocities, and the angular velocities from the <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> measured joints.
The rotations are provided using the 6DoF representation. The output of the network is the <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> joint rotations <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the local reference frame, expressed in 6DoF.
The model is designed to output a frame of 41 samples.
For sequences larger than 41 samples, it uses an overlap of 20 samples between successive frames.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since we require the model to output joint angles <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the global frame, we fine-tune the model using our training datasets to output global rotation angles.
We use the weights provided by the BoDiffusion authors to initialise fine-tuning.
We also tune the model to use only joint rotations and angular velocities for CFG by training the model on a subset of samples with zeroed out joint locations and velocities.
During inverse-guidance-based inference, we can similarly zero out the location and velocity CFG inputs to the diffusion network.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This model has about 22M parameters. Finetuning is done using the DDPM framework for <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1000</annotation></semantics></math> steps.
Inference is done for <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=50</annotation></semantics></math> steps using DDIM, for both pure CFG-based guidance as well as <span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">InPose</span>’s inverse guidance.
We used an Nvidia RTX Titan GPU for training for 2 days with a batch size of 256.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inverse Guidance:</span>
For the <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>GDM-based inverse guidance term <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{r^{t}}\log p_{t}(l_{m}|r^{t}_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{r^{t}}\log p_{t}(l_{m}|r^{t}_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, we used an additional scale parameter, which we found was useful in improving performance for all metrics.
Increasing this term led to minor increases in MPJPE performance at the cost of worse MPJRE error.
Secondly, since the positive definite matrix <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is very large, its usage substantially slows down run-time when its inverse is computed.
We found that setting <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\hat{r}^{t}_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> to simply the identity matrix doesn’t degrade performance with the benefit of faster run-time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>More Results</h2>

<figure id="A3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A3.T2.st1" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">(a) </span>Results with Upper Body shape variation (Protocol 2) (<math id="A3.T2.st1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math> is better)</figcaption>
<div id="A3.T2.st1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:48.5pt;vertical-align:-22.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-100.9pt,12.3pt) scale(0.663195758410936,0.663195758410936) ;">
<table id="A3.T2.st1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Algorithm</span></th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="4">Default shape</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="4">Upper body <math id="A3.T2.st1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 1.4</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="4">Arms <math id="A3.T2.st1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 1.4</annotation></semantics></math>, Torso <math id="A3.T2.st1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 0.7</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">MPJPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">MPJRE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">UPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r">LPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">MPJPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">MPJRE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">UPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r">LPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">MPJPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">MPJRE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">UPE</th>
<th id="A3.T2.st1.2.1.2.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">LPE</th></tr>
</thead>
